WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.280
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Mark from San Antonio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:05.640
Texas. Mark from San Antonio,&nbsp;
Welcome to The David Pakman Show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.120
Mark, please unmute yourself and&nbsp;
then I'll be able to hear you.&nbsp;

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:18.780
Speaker 5: Hey, David, quick question. You know,&nbsp;
I know the the loan forgiveness thing is kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.700
sketchy right now. It's up in the air. But really,&nbsp;
why do you think there's no appetite from Congress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:29.280
to have any kind of legislation around, you&nbsp;
know, either capping the amount that tuition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:35.220
can be raised a year because it's exploding or&nbsp;
even taxing, you know, these endowments? I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.960
Harvard alone, I think, grew their endowment&nbsp;
during COVID by $30 billion. All of this is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.340
you know, essentially PAC money. You know, the&nbsp;
CEO of the Harvard Management Company is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.540
highest paid person associated with the with&nbsp;
the school. I mean, why do you think there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.560
no appetite to go legislate that? I mean, I&nbsp;
can understand the private loan side of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.760
you know, very much in a lot of bed with&nbsp;
with Congress. But, you know, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.669
universities, by and large, really can't donate&nbsp;
to any kind of, you know, political affiliation.&nbsp;

00:01:01.669 --> 00:01:06.060
Speaker 1: So I don't want to be defeatist. And&nbsp;
I know when I give you the reasons why we aren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.500
seeing those things, people will write to me and&nbsp;
they'll say, David, if you don't believe it can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:15.600
done, then of course it won't happen. You're going&nbsp;
to, you know, take the wind out of the sails. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:21.480
going to tell you why it hasn't been done for now&nbsp;
and why it's a difficult thing to do, putting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:27.960
limit on how much tuition can be increased.&nbsp;
I don't see how you could possibly pass that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:35.220
Republicans would say you're telling institutions&nbsp;
how they need to, you know, price their product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.480
or service, which of course, is done. There are&nbsp;
there are industries that are price regulated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:46.200
but the opposition to that would be absolutely&nbsp;
off the charts in order to tax the endowments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.080
of these institutions. Essentially, what you're&nbsp;
talking about is a wealth tax. I mean, remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.180
that we already have different types of taxes&nbsp;
for different types of financial transactions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:03.600
and some have no tax. But to say we're simply&nbsp;
going to tax the holdings is a sort of wealth tax.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.060
And I just don't see any way in hell that right&nbsp;
now you get something like that passed doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.080
mean that these are bad ideas or we shouldn't try.&nbsp;
I'm not saying activists shouldn't be calling for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.800
these things, but to answer the question of why&nbsp;
hasn't it happened, it's because these would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:22.260
such difficult things to get consensus for from&nbsp;
the current House and Senate of the United States.&nbsp;

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:27.600
Speaker 5: All right. Well, thank you. Quick&nbsp;
drummer question. I'm a drummer myself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.040
You know, I know you're a big fan of Josh Freese,&nbsp;
but as far as a player, you know, who do you try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:36.060
to emulate the most or stylistically sound like?
Speaker 1: I don't think I have any particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:41.820
drummer I'm trying to emulate. I mean, I think&nbsp;
I, you know, study so many different drummers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:47.100
from so many different genres and time periods.&nbsp;
But I couldn't really say there's any, any one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:52.080
any one person. I mean, you know, it's sort&nbsp;
of like you're going to learn things from like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:57.780
Carter Beaufort, from Dave MATTHEWS Band that&nbsp;
are unique to Carter, and they're going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:02.100
completely different from the things that&nbsp;
you'll pick up from like a Steve Gadd or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:06.960
you know, from watching old Buddy Rich videos&nbsp;
or whatever the case may be. So I think it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:12.300
sort of consume everything sort of approach.
Speaker 5: Yeah. I mean, I started off as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.120
punk rock drummer, took a break from it for&nbsp;
a while, and when I came back, it was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.180
Oh, I can't play the fast anymore. And so&nbsp;
then I started kind of listening to some,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.229
you know, like you've mentioned Steve&nbsp;
Gadd and composer and all those guys.&nbsp;

00:03:21.229 --> 00:03:25.080
Speaker 1: Oh, that's interesting. So you after&nbsp;
more after a break. Like you think physically you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:28.423
can no longer play as fast.
Speaker 5: Correct? Yeah.&nbsp;

00:03:28.423 --> 00:03:31.860
Speaker 1: Well, how old were you when you took&nbsp;
a break? And how old were you when you restarted?&nbsp;

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:37.440
Speaker 5: Person.
Speaker 1: Oh, boy. You just cut out. I heard 30%.&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.720
Speaker 4: Start.
Speaker 5: I started around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:45.120
12 years old and I played up until I was 18, 19,&nbsp;
you know, sold my gear, went to college and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.140
put it back up, I want to say 27.
Speaker 1: Wow. And then you had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.120
diminished, diminished physical capacity.
Speaker 5: Right. Jeez. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.200
foot speed was kind of gone. And I'm&nbsp;
sure if I committed to it and, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.960
really tried, I could get some of&nbsp;
those chops back. But also it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.380
just like a complete disinterest in it.
Speaker 1: Fair. Fair. All right. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:09.000
we're going to look into it strongly. Okay.&nbsp;
Thanks very much. Appreciate the call.

